## Example usage (`App.tsx`)

```tsx
import React from "react";
import { CheckoutPage } from "./CheckoutPage";
import { CartItem, DeliveryAddress } from "./types";

const sampleItems: CartItem[] = [
  {
    id: "1",
    name: "Wireless Headphones",
    imageUrl: "https://via.placeholder.com/56",
    unitPrice: 79.99,
    quantity: 1,
  },
  {
    id: "2",
    name: "USB-C Charging Cable",
    imageUrl: "https://via.placeholder.com/56",
    unitPrice: 12.5,
    quantity: 2,
  },
];

export const App: React.FC = () => {
  const handleBackToCart = () => {
    console.log("Navigate back to cart");
  };

  const handlePay = async (address: DeliveryAddress) => {
    console.log("Processing payment with address:", address);
    await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1000));
  };

  return (
    <CheckoutPage
      items={sampleItems}
      onBackToCart={handleBackToCart}
      onPay={handlePay}
      currency="USD"
    />
  );
};

export default App;
```

### Notes
- `CheckoutPage` is fully controlled via props (`items`, `onBackToCart`, `onPay`), so you can plug it into any router/state management (Redux, Context, etc.) by wiring those callbacks.
- Validation is basic (required fields + email format) — extend `validateAddress` for postal code/country-specific rules if needed.
- `onPay` can return a Promise; the Pay button shows a "Processing..." state and surfaces thrown errors.
- Layout is responsive: it stacks to a single column under 760px.
